Nara mixed river mud, chimney soot, and one bright tear. She formed a lamp with walls thin as a moth’s wing. The Moon placed inside it a blue ember, warm as breath. “Carry it east,” she said. “Open it only beyond the last shadow.”

Nara walked past sleeping wells and trees dreaming of leaves. But before the far ridge, she heard her village crying: children lost, elders afraid, animals shivering in their stalls. She held the lamp tighter, and pity cracked it.

The ember flew out laughing. It painted clouds rose, filled wheat with gold, woke rivers into silver, and taught birds their songs.

The Moon bowed. “Darkness ends wherever mercy opens.”

Since then, sunrise is a broken lamp blessing the world.

